# THE TESTNET GATEWAY'S APEX. Loaded instead of `traefik.env` when the compose
# project sets CF_TRAEFIK_ENV=traefik.testnet (see compose/testnet.env).
#
# It is a COPY of traefik.env with two values changed, and the prose below is
# that file's — kept rather than trimmed, because every argument in it applies
# here identically and a summarised copy is how two files stop agreeing.
#
# The two values that differ, and nothing else:
#   CF_WEB_APEX  = testnet.cloudsforge.online   (was cloudsforge.localtest.me)
#   CF_API_HOST  = api.testnet.cloudsforge.online
#
# WHY `testnet.` AS AN APEX PREFIX WORKS WITH NO CODE CHANGE, AND THE ONE WAY IT
# COULD STOP. `cloudsforgeHosts()` derives the apex from the browser's own
# hostname by stripping a leading label ONLY when that label is in `KNOWN_SUBS`
# (ui/packages/ui/src/index.tsx:160). At `testnet.cloudsforge.online` the first
# label is `testnet`, which is not a registry subdomain, so nothing is stripped
# and the apex resolves to itself; at `hub.testnet.cloudsforge.online` the first
# label IS `hub`, so it strips to `testnet.cloudsforge.online` and every sibling
# host composes correctly. Verified against the real module, not assumed.
#
# THE FAILURE MODE, NAMED SO IT IS NOT DISCOVERED: the day any surface takes the
# subdomain `testnet`, `KNOWN_SUBS` gains `testnet`, and the BARE APEX
# `testnet.cloudsforge.online` starts stripping its own first label — resolving
# every link on the testnet front page to `cloudsforge.online`, which is
# MAINNET. A test environment silently linking users to production is the worst
# shape this defect could take. `scripts/check-apex-prefix.py` fails on it.
#
# gateway (Traefik) — no secrets.
#
# ACME credentials for a DNS-01 challenge go in a per-provider env file that is
# NOT committed and NOT this one; the gateway is the only container that should
# ever hold them, which is the entire argument for per-service env files.
TZ=UTC

# The host the public API answers on. Every router in
# gateway/dynamic/public-api.yml matches `Host({{ env "CF_API_HOST" }})`.
#
# IT HAS TO BE DEFINED HERE, in the env_file the gateway actually loads.
# It was not, and the failure is silent rather than loud: with the variable
# unset the template renders an EMPTY host and every public router becomes
# `Host(``) && PathPrefix(...)`, which is a valid rule that matches no request
# ever sent. Traefik logs no error. The whole public API is simply dead, and
# the only symptom is a 404 from the catch-all.
#
# It went unnoticed because the verification recipe in README.md passes
# `-e CF_API_HOST=api.example.com` on the command line — so the check supplied
# the value the deployment does not, and proved something the deployment could
# not do.
#
# ── IT MUST BE A SUBDOMAIN OF CF_WEB_APEX, AND IT WAS NOT ─────────────────────
#
# This used to be `api.cloudsforge.online` — "the production host from the CORS
# allowlist; a local stack wants api.localhost" — while `CF_WEB_APEX` below is
# `cloudsforge.localtest.me`. So the whole public API was routed on a hostname
# NOTHING IN THIS ESTATE RESOLVES, and the address the estate's own registry
# declares for it answered 404:
#
#     https://api.cloudsforge.localtest.me/v1/rates   -> 404, before this change
#
# `api` is a REGISTERED SURFACE — `ui/packages/ui/src/surfaces.ts`, subdomain
# `api`, "The public v1 surface" — so `cloudsforgeHosts().api` on any page this
# gateway serves is `https://api.<apex>`, and that is the door the SDK, the
# developer portal and every third-party example compose. Pinning the router to a
# different apex is the same class of defect as the empty `Host(``)` this file's
# comment above describes, one step less invisible: it fails on the right host
# instead of on none.
#
# The two are kept in step by `scripts/estate-up.sh`, which derives this from
# `CF_WEB_APEX` and refuses to start when the two files disagree — the same
# preflight that already guards the apex itself. Overriding it for a real
# deployment is still one line, and a deployment whose API host is genuinely not
# under its web apex sets it here and the preflight says so out loud.
CF_API_HOST=api.testnet.cloudsforge.online

# The apex the FIFTEEN BROWSER SURFACES are served on.
#
# Read by gateway/dynamic/estate-web.yml (every surface router) and by
# gateway/dynamic/policy.yml (the CORS allowlist block for this apex). It is the
# same variable docker-compose.estate.yml interpolates into
# IDENTITY_HANDOFF_ORIGINS, and the two must agree or a browser signs in at Hub
# and cannot hand off to any other surface.
#
# `cloudsforge.localtest.me` is a PUBLIC wildcard resolving to 127.0.0.1, so a
# dev estate needs no /etc/hosts entry and no sudo: hub.cloudsforge.localtest.me,
# nimbus.cloudsforge.localtest.me and the rest all reach loopback. That is what
# makes the production shape — `cloudsforgeHosts()` deriving every sibling host
# by stripping a known subdomain — testable on a laptop at all.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE AND IT MEANS "SERVE NO SURFACES". estate-web.yml
# wraps every router in `{{ if env "CF_WEB_APEX" }}` precisely so that an unset
# value produces NO routers rather than fifteen whose rule reads Host(``) — the
# failure this file's CF_API_HOST comment above exists to describe, which Traefik
# does not log and which looks exactly like a 404 from a catch-all.
CF_WEB_APEX=testnet.cloudsforge.online

# ── THE CERTIFICATE LINE IS GONE FROM BOTH FILES, NOT JUST THIS ONE ──────────
#
# `CF_TLS_DEFAULT_CERT=estate` was here, with a long argument for why this file
# must NOT copy mainnet's `origin`: a Cloudflare Origin leaf covers
# `*.cloudsforge.online` and a wildcard matches one label, so it could never
# cover `hub.testnet.cloudsforge.online`.
#
# That argument is retired rather than answered. MAINNET NO LONGER SERVES AN
# ORIGIN LEAF EITHER — the tunnel reaches the gateway over plain HTTP on the
# `tunnel` entrypoint, so no hop asks for a Cloudflare-issued certificate. Both
# environments serve `estate.crt`, which is what this environment always served,
# and `gateway/dynamic/tls.yml` names it directly instead of through a variable
# with one possible value.
#
# So this is one FEWER line that must not be copied from the other file. The
# remaining three are `CF_WEB_APEX`, `CF_API_HOST` and the three chain upstreams.


# ── THE CHAIN'S TWO UPSTREAMS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Read by `gateway/dynamic/estate-web.yml`, which routes `rpc.<apex>` and
# `p2p.<apex>` to them. Both are wrapped in `{{ if env … }}` there, so an unset
# value produces NO ROUTER rather than one pointed at an empty URL — the same
# shape, and for the same reason, as CF_WEB_APEX above. `scripts/surface-routes.py`
# check 6 fails when a variable that directory reads is missing from this file or
# from its mainnet twin, so an environment cannot lose one of these silently.
#
# ── THESE TWO ARE THE SAME IN BOTH FILES TODAY, AND THAT IS A FACT ABOUT THE
#    ESTATE RATHER THAN A COPY-PASTE ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THERE IS ONE CHAIN. `hearth/docker-compose.testnet.yml` — a seed and two miners
# on chain id 7412 — is the only EMBER network running anywhere, and BOTH compose
# projects already follow it: `docker-compose.estate.yml:971` gives the indexer
# `${EMBER_RPC_URL:-http://host.docker.internal:8545}` in either project, because
# the default is in the file rather than in an environment. So the gateway
# agreeing with the indexer means writing the same node here, and disagreeing
# would mean the public endpoint and the estate's own index reporting two
# different chains.
#
# THE DAY A MAINNET CHAIN EXISTS, `traefik.env` IS THE FILE THAT CHANGES, and it
# is the one line in this pair that must not be copied from the other: a mainnet
# apex publishing a testnet node answers every question correctly and every one of
# them about the wrong chain. Nothing here can detect that — the two nodes speak
# the same protocol — which is why it is written down instead.
#
# 8545 is Hearth's Ethereum JSON-RPC (`hearth/node/src/params.js:327`), NOT 8645,
# which is the REST API and the legacy `{method:'getinfo'}` RPC on the same node.
# ── THE PORTS DIFFER FROM traefik.env, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT ────────────
#
# These three used to read 8545/8648/8645 — BYTE-IDENTICAL to `traefik.env`. The
# long note above explains why that was once correct ("THERE IS ONE CHAIN"), and
# it has stopped being correct: the testnet estate now has its OWN seed, brought
# up by `compose/docker-compose.hearth-seed-testnet.yml` on 8745/8845/8848.
#
# Left unchanged, the testnet gateway would have routed rpc./p2p./mining traffic
# to the MAINNET-apex estate's node — two apexes serving one chain, and a miner
# pointed at testnet crediting blocks on the other environment's ledger. That is
# the failure the header above predicts for the reverse direction, and nothing
# can detect it from outside: both nodes speak the same protocol and both answer
# correctly, about the wrong chain.
CF_RPC_UPSTREAM=http://host.docker.internal:8745

# 8648 is the WebSocket P2P transport, under `HEARTH_P2P_WS`, path `/p2p`. It is a
# contract with hearth and not a free choice: 8646 is the raw-TCP peer port a
# tunnel cannot carry, and 8647/8649 are already the two miners' REST ports on
# this host (`hearth/docker-compose.testnet.yml`).
CF_P2P_WS_UPSTREAM=http://host.docker.internal:8848

# The mining work API — the REST port (8645), not the JSON-RPC port (8545). See
# the long note on this variable in `traefik.env`; the same node serves both, and
# `CF_RPC_UPSTREAM` above cannot reach `/mining/*` at any path.
CF_MINING_UPSTREAM=http://host.docker.internal:8845

# ── WHICH EMBER CHAIN THIS ESTATE IS, AND THEREFORE WHETHER IT HAS A FAUCET ───
#
# `testnet`, and this is the environment where that word buys something: it is
# the one that publishes `network.<apex>/v1` to the faucet. See the long note on
# this variable in `traefik.env` and the longer one beside the router in
# `gateway/dynamic/estate-web.yml`.
#
# THIS IS THE THIRD VALUE THAT MUST NOT BE COPIED FROM THE OTHER FILE, beside
# `CF_WEB_APEX`, `CF_API_HOST` and the three chain upstreams. Copying mainnet's
# `mainnet` here removes the faucet from the only estate that can have one, and
# the symptom is a drip form that renders disabled saying the faucet did not
# answer — indistinguishable, to a reader, from the faucet being down.
CF_EMBER_NETWORK=testnet
